Hi Kevin,

A couple things seem off:
1. How are alpha and beta 0-indexed? Stan is 1-indexed.
2. Some of the Rhats are NaN. In all the runs there are NaN values, but positive neff.

Is there a post processing step that you're taking?

Daniel

On Feb 6, 2017, at 5:35 AM, Kevin D. <kev...@gmail.com> wrote:

> Thanks a lot for the replies.
>
> To Bob: I understand that when the simpler model doesn't explain the data well the posterior might be more difficult to sample from, but in this case the simple model seems to do a pretty good job: when a quadratic or cubic term is added to the scale's polynomial, the inferred coefficients are essentially zero. The number of leapfrog steps might be having an influence. Lastly, I've run it quite a few times, and the times I posted above seem to be a decent indication.
>
> To Andrew: My application is distance vs time — from a plot, it seems like there's a linear trend for large distances, but this isn't the case for small distances, where there seems to be a bit of curvature. If polynomial regression is a bad idea in this case, my issue could very well just be a resulting anomaly. Regarding the scale of the coefficients, in this case the only parameters that aren't on the unit scale are those unique to the complex model.
>
> I'm not hugely concerned about all this though — it clearly has to do with a combination of my data and model, and I've already got a decent answer. I guess my confusion is just the following: the complex model seems to fit the data nicely (and samples quickly), but it isn't 'using' the parameters it has over-and-above the simpler model, so I would think that the simpler model is good enough, and therefore should sample just as quickly.
