On Monday, February 6, 2017 at 3:55:04 PM UTC-5, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> You should be worried about NaN R-hats.  The only way
> that usually happens is if a parameter never moves, which
> usually means it's somehow accidentally set to a constant
> in the model somewhere.  Or it has such extreme underflow
> problems that it winds up being zero everywhere.

Not sure if it's applicable in this case (I haven't gone through the model), however, in general there are some cases of NaN R-hats that are _not_ problematic. For example, if you have a Cholesky factor as a parameter then you will get NaN (or maybe NA if in R) for the Rhats of the redundant elements that don't get directly estimated. But if something like that isn't applicable here then NaN would definitely be a cause for concern.

Jonah

