> Yeah I agree that having NaNs that aren't actually red flags is not so cool. But I'm not sure if setting to 1 is better.   It would be weird (if not misleading) to print an Rhat of 1 if the identical parameter on the other side of the diagonal (the one that's actually estimated) has a high Rhat.
>
> I think the NaNs are coming from Stan (rather than being changed by RStan), so if this behavior is going to change in any way it probably makes sense to do it at the Stan level rather than at the interface level.
>
> Jonah
>
> On Mon, Feb 6, 2017 at 4:57 PM Andrew Gelman <gel...@stat.columbia.edu> wrote:
