> To Bob: That makes sense — now that I think about it, NaN R-hats shouldn't be showing up. In fact, when I extract the samples for 'alpha[3]' and compute the R-hat value by hand (I tried reimplementing the Stan source in Python as well as following Andrew's description in the Handbook of MCMC) I get a valid answer, just like the other R-hats. If I print the samples, they also seem reasonable to me. However, for the other parameters there's a slight discrepancy between the values I get and those that Stan produces, and although I've checked my code a few times I can't figure out why (I've tried including/excluding warm-up samples).
>
> Regarding the cubic coefficient, I guess I was just looking at the description of the inferred samples given by Stan (so the mean and quantiles). But here, for example, is a description of a set of samples (incl. warm-ups) for 'alpha[3]' — the cubic coefficient of the location polynomial which I regarded as "close to zero", and which Stan says has a NaN R-hat value:
>
> count    4000.000000
> mean       -0.000052
> std         0.000041
> min        -0.000286
> 25%        -0.000066
> 50%        -0.000045
> 75%        -0.000031
> max         0.000219
>
> Also, here are the R-hats I computed for another run in which the location and scale polynomials had degrees 4 and 2 respectively (the simpler, slower model); Stan's R-hats are in parentheses:
>
> alpha[0]: 1.0436 (1.09)
> alpha[1]: 1.0228 (1.04)
> alpha[2]: 1.0229 (1.03)
> alpha[3]: 1.0204 (NaN)
>
> Thanks,
> Kevin
>
