So it looks like the NaN issue was an artifact of forgetting to standardise. On that note though: I fixed my implementation of the R-hat computation (I was using unbiased variance estimators; and to answer your last question I've been splitting the post-warmup samples from multiple chains), and even though the values I computed agreed with those given by Stan, I still didn't get issues computing the value for 'alpha[3]', even when Stan returned NaN. I can only guess that NumPy's variance computations use greater precision than Boost's.

Standardisation doesn't exactly make my main problem go away — when I originally encountered the slow sampling problem, I was already standardising both the predictors and the outcomes — but in the light of the previous answers it does make things clearer. I've pasted some new timed inferences below, and although they appear to suffer from the same simple/complex model issue as before (standardisation does speed things up, so I've increased the size of the data sample from N = 500 to N = 2000), I guess there's a bit of evidence there for your suggestion that the simpler model doesn't explain the data as well, and therefore has more uncertainty (the slightly higher std. dev. for 'beta[1]' and R-hat values seem to support that?). I think my mistake has been underestimating the effect of coefficients like 'beta[3] = 0.04'. In the end though, I still find the difference in sampling times quite surprising.

Thanks for all the help,
Kevin

