> On Feb 16, 2017, at 6:36 PM, Stefano <mangiol...@gmail.com> wrote:
>
> Hello Community,
>
> I have a regression model. In my design instead of inferring angular coefficient with many points, I do the other way around (a point called GAMMA with many angular coefficients).
>
> Said that my problem can be reduces to this:
>
> If I predict gamma[1] and gamma[2] from two independent datasets, but I know they should be "linked" to the same distribution. so I put gamma ~ normal(mu, sigma)

I don't know what you mean by predict or linked, but I understand the
Stan sampling statement.

> The problem that two points are not enough to calculate a normal distribution. And there is not convergence.

Put a proper prior on mu and sigma and then you'll get a proper
posterior and shouldn't have convergence issues.  Cauchy prior
may still be too fat.

>
> Does this make sense? Is it something illogical to do?

It's more useful in a hierarchical modeling setting where there
are other groups from which to estimate hyper-priors.

- Bob

From u83b63066408210ec at groups.google.com  Fri Feb 17 19:48:05 2017
From: u83b63066408210ec at groups.google.com (Pedro Carvalho)
Date: Fri, 17 Feb 2017 19:48:05 +0000
Subject: Different results depending on whether I apply a function in the transformed parameters or model
Message-ID: <qXM2yFExvnc.tlyYfihcBwAJ@stan-users.groups.google.com>
Archived-At: <https://groups.google.com/g/stan-users/c/qXM2yFExvnc/m/tlyYfihcBwAJ>
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset="utf-8"
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

So I'm fitting a model that goes like this:

data {
    int <lower = 1> time;

    int <lower = 0> successes [time];
    int <lower = 0> total [time];
}

parameters {
    real <lower = 0, upper = 1> initial
    real <lower = 0, upper = 1> asymptote;
    real <lower = 0> scale;
}

transformed parameters {
    real theta_logit [time];
    for (i in 1:time) {
        theta_logit = logit (asymptote + (initial - asymptote) / (1 + (i - 1) / scale));
    }
}

model {
    initial ~ beta (1, 1);
    asymptote ~ beta (1, 1);
    scale ~ gamma (1, 1);
    for (t in 1:time) {
        if (total [t] > 0) {
            successes [t] ~ binomial_logit (total [t], theta_logit [t]);
        }
    }
}

It does not converge, and the results are all wrong. However, if I move the "logit" function from the transformed parameters section to the model section:

data {
    int <lower = 1> time;

    int <lower = 0> successes [time];
    int <lower = 0> total [time];
}

parameters {
    real <lower = 0, upper = 1> initial
    real <lower = 0, upper = 1> asymptote;
    real <lower = 0> scale;
}

transformed parameters {
    real theta_logit [time];
    for (i in 1:time) {
        theta_logit = asymptote + (initial - asymptote) / (1 + (i - 1) / scale);
    }
}

model {
    initial ~ beta (1, 1);
    asymptote ~ beta (1, 1);
    scale ~ gamma (1, 1);
    for (t in 1:time) {
        if (total [t] > 0) {
            successes [t] ~ binomial_logit (total [t], logit (theta_logit [t]));
        }
    }
}

Then that converges and works wonderfully.

What's up with that?

