Hi Bob,

Thanks very much for your response. A few questions:

1) What do you mean by "you want to see where the divergences are at"? I'm not sure how to operationalize that phrase.
2) I'm using the product of a gamma with a normal to generate a t. How do I think about comparing the "scales" of these different distributions? Is it better to draw uniforms and transform via inverse cdfs?
3) Suppose that my prior distributions are independent and I'm using the QR decomposition on covariate matrices. What else could I do to eliminate correlation in the posterior?

Thanks,
David

On Monday, February 20, 2017 at 12:16:42 PM UTC-6, Bob Carpenter wrote:
> The pairs plot by default sets up two chains above the diagonal
> and two chains below the diagonal.  So all this is saying is that
> they're happening on a subset of the chains.  You want to see where
> the divergences are at to see if you can learn anything.
>
> The ideal pairs plot will look like a bunch of bulls-eyes of
> independent unit-scaled posteriors.  Anything that smacks of high
> correlation or varying scales can be a problem for adaptation or
> sampling.
>
> - Bob
>
> > On Feb 20, 2017, at 12:57 PM, David <djtma...@gmail.com> wrote:
