> 1) What do you mean by "you want to see where the divergences are at"? I'm not sure how to operationalize that phrase.

The points at which divergent transitions arise are
highlighted in the plots.  Or you can get them directly
in RStan using this:

https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/rstan/vignettes/stanfit-objects.html

> 2) I'm using the product of a gamma with a normal to generate a t. How do I think about comparing the "scales" of these different distributions? Is it better to draw uniforms and transform via inverse cdfs?

Why do that rather than have the Student-t itself?  Multiplicative
paramters are bad news as they tend to lead to banana-like posteriors,
which are terrible --- no good adaptation parameters given that
curvature varies around the parameter.

> 3) Suppose that my prior distributions are independent and I'm using the QR decomposition on covariate matrices. What else could I do to eliminate correlation in the posterior?

That's a question for Ben---you may want to pull it out in
its own message to get the right people's attention.

- Bob

