Hi, I'm working with products (beverages) daily sales divided into groups (pet sodas, red wine, blonde beer, ecc.) . I'm considering a single group for now (pet sodas), with 47 products.

I don't want to fit a different model for each product nor fit a single model for all the products, but I want to share information across products.

What I did is considering 2 groups brand (I have 3 of them) and sugar level (3), basically it is a nested model since for each brand I could have every sugar level (for one brand I don't, since there is no 'light' version for the sugar).

My covariates are the prices of each product, plus 18 covariates from day of the week to holiday to weather. I have 47+18 covariates, 65, for 729 observations for each product.

As regards the 18 covariates I put a fixed effect on them, so that their contribution to each product sales is the same for all the products.

As regards the other 47 I put mixed effects varying by group and mixed effects varying by sugar level. I constrained this to be both positive, since I expect that whichever the product I expect an increase in its price to positively affect 46 products and negatively affect the product itself. That's why I introduce another effect, for each product, which multiplies its price plus other 3 covariates (intercept, number of promotions and a stockout flag).

The model then becomes:

$\lambda_{tijk} = X_t*\beta + Z_t*(brand_j+sugar_k) + P_{ti}*eta_i$
$Y_{tijk}=Poi(exp(\lambda_{tijk}))$

First of all I was wondering whether it was possible to write it in rstanarm, since I found documentation about simpler univariate models (here I have a couple of issues I wouldn't know how to face, i.e. the i and t indexes and the product specific regressors).

The model in stan looks like this:

data {
int<lower=1> N;
int<lower=1> K;
int<lower=1> R;

int<lower=1> I; // number of products
int<lower=1> B; // number of brands
int<lower=1> S; // number of sugars/flavours
matrix[N,K] X; //fixed effects matrix
matrix[N,R] Z; //mixed effects matrix (the same for brand and sugar)
int Y[N,I];    //Sales
vector[K] zerobeta; //Prior mean on fixed effects
vector[4] zeroeta;  //Prior mean on product specific effects
vector[R] zeromix; //Prior mean on mixed effects for sugar and brand
int<lower=1,upper=B> brand[I];
int<lower=1,upper=S> sugar[I];
row_vector[4] Zeta[N,I]; //matrix for product specific effects
}

parameters {
//vector<lower=0>[I] phi;
vector[K] beta;
vector[4] eta[I]; // random, product specific effects
vector<lower=0>[R] b1[B]; // brand raneffs
vector<lower=0>[R] b2[S]; // sugar raneffs
corr_matrix[K] Omega_beta;
corr_matrix[4] Omega_eta;
corr_matrix[R] Omega_brand;
corr_matrix[R] Omega_sugar;
vector<lower=0>[K] sigma_be;
vector<lower=0>[R] sigma_br;
vector<lower=0>[R] sigma_su;
vector<lower=0>[4] sigma_et;

}

transformed parameters {
cov_matrix[K] Sigma_beta;
cov_matrix[R] Sigma_brand;
cov_matrix[R] Sigma_sugar;
cov_matrix[4] Sigma_eta;
Sigma_beta = quad_form_diag(Omega_beta,sigma_be);
Sigma_brand = quad_form_diag(Omega_brand,sigma_br);
Sigma_sugar = quad_form_diag(Omega_sugar,sigma_su);
Sigma_eta = quad_form_diag(Omega_eta,sigma_et);
}
model {

 beta ~ multi_normal(zerobeta , Sigma_beta);
 sigma_be ~ cauchy(0,5);
 sigma_et ~ cauchy(0,5);
 sigma_br ~ cauchy(0,5);
 sigma_su ~ cauchy(0,5);
 Omega_beta ~ lkj_corr(1);
 Omega_eta ~ lkj_corr(1);
 Omega_sugar ~ lkj_corr(1);
 Omega_brand ~ lkj_corr(1);
 eta ~ multi_normal(zeroeta, Sigma_eta);
 b1 ~ multi_normal(zeromix, Sigma_brand) ;
 b2 ~ multi_normal(zeromix, Sigma_sugar);
 for(i in 1:N){
// phi ~ cauchy(0,5);
  for(l in 1:47){
 real lambda;

 lambda = X[i,]*beta+
 Zeta[i,l]*eta[l]+
 Z[i,]*(b1[brand[l]]+b2[sugar[l]]);

 Y[i,l] ~ poisson_log(lambda);
}
}

}

I specified positive definite symmetric matrices for all correlation and covariance matrices as initializations. As regards priors for the means I put everything around 0.

It is very slow, even when running 100 iterations (more than an hour). I don't know what to try, maybe:

optimizing the stan code in some way? (is there some vectorizing in the model part?)
putting hyperpriors on the mixed effects means?
Maybe there's a problem in the model specification regarding eta?

I've been struggling with this for quite a while and I kindly ask for some help..

Thank you

